WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:25.714
Controls and thermostats are the brain of the system. When they fail, nothing
works right.

00:00:25.714 --> 00:00:51.429
System diagnostics using gauge readings: both pressures high = overcharge or
non-condensables. Both pressures low = undercharge. Low suction high discharge =
airflow restriction. High suction low discharge = compressor weakness. Normal

00:00:51.429 --> 00:01:17.143
Here are the key terms. Write these down.

00:01:17.143 --> 00:01:42.857
Non-Condensables: Air or nitrogen in the system. Takes up condenser space
raising head pressure.

00:01:42.857 --> 00:02:08.571
Here's why this matters on the job: Pattern recognition with gauge readings is
the core diagnostic skill. The faster you can read the pattern the faster you
solve the problem.

00:02:08.571 --> 00:02:34.286
Watch out for these: Always verify airflow FIRST. And — Both pressures tell a
story together

00:02:34.286 --> 00:03:00.000
Take a minute before you move on. What's the one thing from this lesson you need
to remember on a service call?

